# sky130A — open reference extraction deck for vyges-extract
#
# Capacitance is calibrated against the foundry-reference extractor (OpenROAD **OpenRCX**, nom
# corner, rules.openrcx.sky130A.nom) across a **set of 10 sky130 blocks**, each scored against
# the SPEF its own OpenLane run produced: CF_UART_APB, counter, fft_top, humidistat_apb,
# mag_phase_apb, rv_plic_lite, spectrum_buffer, spi_host_lite, temp_sensor_apb, xbar_main.
#
# `fft_ctrl_tlul` (14 238 nets) was **held out of every fit** and scored only afterwards:
#
#     ground 0.98x   coupling 1.02x   TOTAL 1.00x
#     per-net median: ground 0.95 (p10 0.73, p90 1.20), coupling 1.04 (p10 0.83, p90 1.24)
#
# Fitted by `correlation/calibrate_coupling.py` then `correlation/calibrate_ground.py`, in that
# order — `shield_k` multiplies OUR coupling, so it cannot be fitted until the coupling column is
# trustworthy, or you are fitting one error against another. See correlation/ground-vs-coupling.md.
#
# ── shield_k — why ground is not just a per-um number ──────────────────────────────────
# Field that terminates on a neighbour is field that did not terminate on ground, so a net's
# grounded cap is reduced by `shield_k * Cc_net`. Without it a deck fitted on a sparse block
# over-states ground on a dense one — the previous deck tracked `counter` to 0.997 and ran 1.37x
# over on a real block, with a +0.94 correlation between a net's coupling-to-ground ratio and its
# error. `Cc_net` here counts a net's coupling from BOTH sides, matching `engine.rs`; expressed
# against coupling counted once the same fraction reads ~0.36.
#
# ── Known bound: the coupling coefficient is right in total, unexplained per mechanism ──
# At minimum spacing the fitted met1 lateral coupling works out to ~0.154 fF/um against ~0.060
# for an ideal sidewall parallel plate and ~0.077 for a fringe-corrected one — **~2.5x a value
# a single sidewall pair should not exceed**. The deck reproduces OpenRCX's per-net coupling
# across eleven blocks anyway, so something real is being counted by a coefficient that does not
# mean what its name says.
#
# The obvious candidate was crossover: this deck defines no `interlayer` coefficients, so
# layer-to-layer coupling is computed as ZERO and the lateral term could be absorbing it. That
# was TESTED and is unresolved, not confirmed. Fitting lateral and crossover jointly
# (`calibrate_coupling.py`, which supports `interlayer` terms) drives every significant
# crossover pair to 0.0 and changes the held-out block by nothing (median 1.04 -> 1.06, slightly
# worse). But that is not evidence crossover is negligible: the two columns are **0.93-0.96
# correlated** on this set (`correlation/collinearity.py`), so the fit cannot separate them.
#
# So: the totals are calibrated and validated; the mechanism is not established. Treat the
# coefficient as an empirical fit over sky130 std-cell digital routing, and do NOT assume it
# travels to a design with markedly different layer usage. Settling it needs a block whose
# crossover-to-lateral ratio differs enough to break the collinearity — which is why a
# non-std-cell held-out block is the next thing worth having, rather than more fitting.
#
# ── Unconstrained layers ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# li1 is UNFITTED (0 constraining nets): sky130 routing uses li1 only for via landings, so no
# li1 wire segment appears anywhere in the set. met4/met5 carry met3's fitted values (187 and 0
# nets). A least-squares fit returns 0.0 for an unconstrained layer, which would silently switch
# that layer off; both scripts refuse coefficients under 200 nets and carry a stated placeholder.
#
# ── Resistance ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Physical, from the sky130_fd_sc_hd nom tech LEF (RESISTANCE RPERSQ). Not calibrated against
# anything; an R correlation pass is separate.
#
# Calibrated to OpenRCX, a reference extractor — NOT to silicon. A sign-off / certified per-fab
# deck is silicon-correlated and NDA, and is never in this repo.
#
# layer  res(ohm/um)  cap(fF/um)  coupling(fF/um)  s_ref(um)
li1      12.8         0.060        0.030            0.17
met1      0.125       0.12494      0.21941          0.14
met2      0.125       0.09676      0.26618          0.14
met3      0.047       0.13365      0.26458          0.30
met4      0.047       0.13365      0.26458          0.30
met5      0.0285      0.13365      0.26458          1.60
via       9.3
# ── couple_shape — the fall-off, taken from characterisation rather than assumed ───────
# Coupling does not fall off as 1/s. These curves are the reference extractor's own
# characterised spacing tables (rules.openrcx.sky130A.nom, `Metal N OVER 0`, coupling column),
# normalised to 1.0 at each layer's first sample so they scale the fitted `coupling` column
# rather than replacing it — the deck still owns the magnitude, characterisation owns the shape.
#
# The gap this closes: met1 retains 0.773 of its minimum-spacing coupling at twice the spacing,
# where 1/s gives 0.500. A 1/s deck fitted on TOTALS therefore ran ~20% low per pair (median
# 0.82, stable across every cutoff) and had to inflate its coefficient to compensate.
# See docs/extract/coupling-mechanism-open.md in vyges-tools-internal.
#
# NOTE these are OpenRCX's numbers, so the deck currently inherits their characterisation of
# sky130 rather than characterising it independently. Fine for correlation, and the reason a
# field-solved table of our own is still wanted for the per-fab claim.
couple_shape li1  0.17:1.0000 0.34:0.6219 0.425:0.5414 0.51:0.4791 0.595:0.4275 0.68:0.3843 0.765:0.3481 0.85:0.3176 1.02:0.2697 1.36:0.2069 1.7:0.1688
couple_shape met1 0.14:1.0000 0.28:0.7734 0.35:0.6550 0.42:0.6001 0.49:0.5448 0.56:0.4892 0.63:0.4376 0.7:0.4157 0.84:0.3715 1.12:0.2860 1.4:0.2500
couple_shape met2 0.14:1.0000 0.28:0.7667 0.35:0.6513 0.42:0.5984 0.49:0.5451 0.56:0.4915 0.63:0.4419 0.7:0.4210 0.84:0.3790 1.12:0.2975 1.4:0.2626
couple_shape met3 0.3:1.0000 0.6:0.7179 0.75:0.6595 0.9:0.6007 1.05:0.5415 1.2:0.4820 1.35:0.4220 1.5:0.3888 1.8:0.3493 2.4:0.2696 3:0.2298
couple_shape met4 0.3:1.0000 0.6:0.7228 0.75:0.6668 0.9:0.6104 1.05:0.5535 1.2:0.4963 1.35:0.4387 1.5:0.4067 1.8:0.3685 2.4:0.2915 3:0.2517
couple_shape met5 1.6:1.0000 3.2:0.5282 4:0.4311 4.8:0.3657 5.6:0.3181 6.4:0.2823 7.2:0.2544 8:0.2319 9.6:0.1976 12.8:0.1545 16:0.1285

couple_cutoff 2.0     # um — ignore lateral coupling beyond this gap
shield_k  0.18081     # grounded cap -= shield_k * Cc_net (both sides) — see above
